<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The five entitlements the jailbroken variant needs, and nothing else.

  Measured as a matrix of seven variants on iOS 26.6.2; two of the results are
  counter-intuitive and are the reason this file is exactly this short:

    - The sandbox escape is the gate for BOTH capabilities. A containerized
      process gets EPERM from proc_listallpids no matter which task-port
      entitlements it carries — adding all three below to a containerized app
      changed nothing at all.
    - Injection then needs EXACTLY task_for_pid-allow. Apple's own
      com.apple.system-task-ports does not work, and neither does
      platform-application. The kernel's task_for_pid check reads the old one.

  So: no-sandbox + no-container make listing possible, task_for_pid-allow makes
  injecting possible on top of that.

  The two runningboard entitlements then decide whether a process may *run*,
  which is a separate axis from whether we may inject into it. iOS suspends an
  app within about a second of it leaving the foreground, and a suspended
  process has no thread scheduled to run injected code in — so without these,
  injecting into an app always times out and this variant stops answering as
  soon as it is not on screen. Daemons are unaffected either way; the system
  does not suspend them, which is why injection worked for them first.

    - primitiveattribute is the whole gate on acquiring an RBSAssertion built
      from raw grants. Verified by decompilation rather than by trial:
      +[RBSAttribute allowedForPrimitiveAttributeForProcessTarget:withError:]
      checks exactly this one key (and refuses the system target outright), and
      RBSCPUAccessGrant then sets RBProcessState.preventSuspend
      unconditionally. Targeting by pid needs nothing further —
      com.apple.runningboard.targetidentities is only for naming a target by
      bundle identifier, which this does not do.
    - process-state is what lets us ask whether a *different* process is
      actually running, so a target that is still suspended fails immediately
      and says so instead of waiting out the injector's 20-second verdict
      budget and reporting a bare timeout. Asking about this process needs no
      entitlement.

  Measurements and addresses:
  Documentations/Evolutions/draft-device-process-assertions.md.

  Nothing else earns a place here. In particular
  com.apple.runningboard.launchprocess is deliberately absent: it would allow
  launching an app that has never run, which is a separate proposal and a
  wider grant than this needs.

  Xcode cannot sign these — no provisioning profile grants them — so a build
  installed by Xcode cannot inject. The variant is signed ad-hoc with these
  entitlements and installed by something that honours them; vphoned preserves
  each binary's existing entitlements when it installs, and a jailbreak
  installer grants them outright.

  ENABLE_APP_SANDBOX is NO for this target on purpose: it would generate
  com.apple.security.app-sandbox, which directly contradicts no-sandbox.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.no-container</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.no-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.runningboard.primitiveattribute</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.runningboard.process-state</key>
	<true/>
	<key>task_for_pid-allow</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
